WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.525
Before May 2022, a domain controller mapped
a certificate to an account by name.

2
00:00:05.525 --> 00:00:11.325
KB5014754 changed the KDC
to want a strong mapping.

3
00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:16.963
The old guide said enforcement began in
February 2025; that was one step of four.

4
00:00:16.963 --> 00:00:19.712
May 2022, Compatibility mode.

5
00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:23.612
April 2023, the Disabled
setting is ignored.

6
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:29.751
February 11, 2025, Full Enforcement for
domain controllers with no value set.

7
00:00:29.751 --> 00:00:34.912
September 9, 2025, the registry
key is no longer honored.

8
00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:38.471
As of October 2026, every
current domain controller

9
00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:41.863
is in Full Enforcement with
no supported way back.

10
00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:48.142
A strong mapping is the SID extension AD
CS adds, the SID in a tagged SAN URL,

11
00:00:48.142 --> 00:00:53.690
which is Intune's form, or an
explicit strong value on the account,

12
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.400
such as issuer and serial
number, SKI or SHA-1 public key.

13
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.338
What Intune profiles need.

14
00:01:00.338 --> 00:01:05.695
It applies to user certificates on all
platforms and to device certificates only

15
00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:10.737
on hybrid joined Windows devices, and the
user or device must be synced from AD.

16
00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:14.079
For SCEP, add a SAN
attribute of type URI with

17
00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:17.700
the on-premises security
identifier variable.

18
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.983
The old guide said Cloud
PKI adds it automatically;

19
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:23.875
Microsoft's instructions
say you must add it.

20
00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:27.172
For PKCS, run the
current connector and set

21
00:01:27.172 --> 00:01:30.563
the SID security extension registry value.

22
00:01:30.563 --> 00:01:34.650
Logon certificates need
their issuing CA in NTAuth.

23
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.925
Pilot with a new profile, because editing
a SCEP profile revokes what it issued.

24
00:01:39.925 --> 00:01:46.638
To find weak certificates, sweep the
KDC's System log for events 39, 40 and 41.

25
00:01:46.638 --> 00:01:51.498
One trap on a mixed estate:
on Server 2008 and 2008 R2,

26
00:01:51.498 --> 00:01:58.326
event 41 means no strong mapping, and
the other two are logged as 48 and 49.

27
00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:01.370
Read the event text before
you classify a hit.
